**RESULT (real data, N=446 IS + 128 OOS shelf-rejection events across 56 distinct trading
days): NO-LIFT on BOTH windows.** IS: signal mean excursion 0.692pt vs null-B (random entry)
0.627pt, lift +0.065pt but p=0.114 (not significant); vs null-A (random-price reject) signal
actually UNDERPERFORMED (-0.117pt, p=0.933). OOS: signal 0.369pt vs null-B 0.390pt (lift
-0.021pt, p=0.652) -- a shelf rejection did not even beat a coin-flip-direction random entry
out of sample. **H2 (does a STRONGER shelf predict a bigger reaction) is NOT just
unsupported, it is INVERTED**: weak shelves show the biggest excursions (IS: weak=0.860 >
mid=0.657 > strong=0.558pt; OOS: same ordering), corr(strength,excursion) is negative in both
windows (-0.112 IS, -0.075 OOS). This is a materially cleaner failure than a generic
no-signal null: if the mechanism were real, MORE volume memory should predict a BIGGER
reaction (as it does, weakly, for LEVEL_MEMORY's swing-pivot levels) -- here it predicts a
SMALLER one, which reads as "big volume nodes are simply high-liquidity zones where price
passes through easily," the opposite of a support/resistance shelf.

